has led some to  suggest that the timelines from hyrule  historia and encyclopedia are not the  real deal and are again the product of  some third party so basically anuma is  hiding the truth from us that tricky  bastard what they don't seem to realize  is that this interview was conducted in  2010 well over a year before hiro  historia was published as much as i love  a good conspiracy theory i have to  appeal to your sense of logic here what  do you think is the more likely scenario  that the zelda staff would publish an  official zelda lore book with an  incorrect timeline made up by some third  party while secretly hiding the real one  or that the secret document anuma is  referring to here is simply one of the  assets they were putting together in  preparation for hyrule historia which  was set to release one and a half years  later i will leave that up for you to  decide now with all that said i do have  to even out the playing field and say  that even though it may not seem that  way given all i've said i actually do  agree to some extent with those who are  critical of the books i think labeling  them as non-canon or trying to say that  they shouldn't be taken seriously is  definitely a step too far but i do share  the belief that the book should not be  taken as a gospel on zelda lore you know  what nintendo agrees with you there's a  good reason why the books include so  many phrases like it is believed or it  is said or it could be the creators of  the games have always made it abundantly  clear that they want to encourage people  to speculate challenge ideas or come up  with their own or look for new  discoveries zelda canon is flexible it  always has been it is shaped by both the  creators of the games as well as the  fans who interpret these stories years  even decades down the line aunuma  himself explains that the gameplay is  always at the forefront of development  and that the stories could be considered  afterthoughts that often fall into place  by chance even the official timeline has  a footnote that states that it is  subject to change based on new  discoveries and the player's imagination  these are all reminders that some  aspects of the lore are not set in stone  and are a continuous puzzle that evolves  over time and yes the books do indeed  contain mistakes and again this really  shouldn't come as a surprise i mean look  at the size of them with this amount of  content spanning across 18 games some of  which were made in the 80s and 90s no  amount of double triple unquadrakin  tuple checking is going to filter out  every oversight some arguments made  against the books are downright petty  pointing out the smallest mistakes in  the print as if it somehow proves the  incompetence of those who put their  hearts and souls into these projects  like this one for example where they  accidentally flipped the fire in the  spirit medallion this has been fixed in  later editions by the way including mine  so it's not even relevant anymore but  there are definitely versions out there  that feature this mistake now look i'm  one of the biggest zelda lore freaks out  there it's become my job pretty much and  even i accidentally mixed up two of the  medallions in one of my videos mistakes  happen it doesn't mean the people who  worked on the books are incompetent or  didn't care and patrick himself can  vouch for this he's well aware of every  mistake and oversight as an editor  working on books of this size you kinda  have to accept the fact that you'll  never get everything right these  projects can't go on forever they have  budgets and deadlines and let's not  forget that every mistake is also one  that nintendo themselves failed to catch  as for inconsistencies in the lore  itself again i have to ask what did you  expect we all know that zelda lore isn't  perfect and the staff will be the first  to admit this a book like hyrule astoria  encyclopedia or creating a champion will  never be able to give you all the  answers and they were never meant to  they are meant to enrich the world of  zelda to tell stories the developers  weren't able to tell in the games  themselves and sometimes these stories  will be speculative it's also worth  considering that sometimes the creators  themselves may not even remember the  original meaning behind something they  wrote years ago which may sound weird  but it's more common than you think take  for example my favorite band muse in  2001 they released a song called plug-in  baby and ever since then fans have been  trying to find out what the lyrics and  its title actually mean in interviews  lead singer matthew bellamy changed up  the story behind the song so many times  that it only became more confusing and  when finally asked by a fan in front of  a live audience his ultimate answer was  i can't remember now where is it i read  i read some book about like i don't know  what it was something to do with um  like uh my playing maybe um  i don't know i can't remember  when a piece of art or content wraps up  creators move on to new things and they  don't always retain every detail behind  the choices they made stories evolve  creators change their mind and the  bigger an ip gets the harder it becomes  to keep it all together and consistent  even the best of the best in the  industry make revisions and amendments  to their work the original story of the  hobbit was changed in later editions to  make it more compatible with the story  of lord of the rings some of the lore  from the original metroid prime was  altered for similar reasons the zelda  games are no different they too contain  inconsistencies retcons contradictions  and localization differences some games  even have two versions like ocarina of  time and majora's mask which have key  differences in some of the dialogue and  especially in their environments so  which version is canon the old one  because it was the original vision of  the creators or the new one where they  may have had the opportunity to rethink  certain lore aspects or make them more  consistent with games that came after  like how they included the happy mask  salesman's backpack in ocarina of time  3d i don't think one option is  automatically inferior to the other both  could and should be able to serve as  evidence to support your theory of  choice so why can't the same thing apply  to the books only sticking to one type  of media has always struck me as a bit  counter-intuitive because there was a  time when printed material held the same  value as the games themselves they  expanded upon all sorts of aspects the  games weren't able to communicate either  because of technical limitations time  restraints or because it would have  padded out the game too much some of the  most interesting parts of breath the  wild story take place in creating a  champion things we would have never  known about if it wasn't for the  existence of this book like the story of  akala citadel the evacuations of central  hyrule and many other interesting  stories related to specific buildings or  characters as i mentioned at the very  beginning for the majority of zelda  history we weren't even sure if nintendo  cared about zelda lore or timelines but  after decades of silence they opened up  their archives and gave us an excessive  amount of information artwork and ideas  we never had access to before i say use  that information and if something  doesn't add up by all means challenge it  create your own theory write a book send  it to nintendo i don't know and maybe  one day when a new game or book comes  out you may just see one of your  theories become a fact so next time  someone references the zelda  encyclopedia or creating a champion  don't simply hand wave it away or tell  them to stick to the games on the flip  side when someone challenges the lore  inside the books it's also worth  listening because they may be onto  something every discovery we make could  potentially shape or inspire future  books games timelines maybe even movies  limiting yourself to only one type of  media one language or one idea goes  against everything zelda lore stands for  people have dug up the most obscure  references localization differences beta  elements and so on some of it from the  games some from the books or the manuals  or even interviews all in search of  ideas and inspiration  in conclusion i feel like there's enough  indications that the zelda team was most  definitely involved to a high enough  degree to consider this a product of  their making and thus they are a canon  edition to the series but in true zelda  fashion their contents are not  indisputable and could be subject to  change and revision in the future to  some degree it already has seeing as the  timeline from hyrule historia was made  obsolete by encyclopedia you may have  noticed that i haven't really touched  upon specific lore details like the  infamous section in the zelda  encyclopedia that talks about majora's  mask being some sort of dream i would  have loved to triple the runtime of this  video and talk about it and oh believe  me it's day is coming but i didn't want  to turn this into a lore video that  wasn't my intention here if i talk about  one contradictory piece of lore i have  to talk about all of them and this  project was already getting out of hand  my goal was to show you more about how  these books came to be and some of the  people behind it if you still believe  the books are not canon even after all  i've said that's fine you do you there  is a thing called headcanon zelda is not  like star wars or lord of the rings  where most things are explained and  fleshed out in great detail the  adventures and backstories are purposely  set up as ancient legends that may or  may not be true with a lot of gaps that  require the imagination of the player  it's an endless puzzle that'll continue  to introduce new pieces to work with and  the day we solve everything is the day  that zelda theories cease to exist let's  hope that day never comes and with that  i have said all i wanted to say on the  matter thank you for watching the video  it goes without saying that a huge huge  thanks goes to patrick thorpe from dark  horse comics who's been at my side for  the past weeks answering questions and  giving amazing insight into the work  that goes into these projects another  shout out goes to rawan who sent me  these amazing miniature manuals of some  of the games since i don't own a lot of  the official manuals physically it  actually came in handy for the video so  thank you very much there's a link to  the etsy store in the description in  case you want to pick up one of these  for yourself as always a big thanks to  my supporters on patreon and my channel  members and say hello to a few newcomers  it's bats invade and raid watermelon  hugo wolf's guard thavest dill pickle  desecrated cake brandon eccle and alex  sp thank you so much for joining also a  special thanks to chap c for gifting me  some of these new memberships now go and  watch me scream like a little girl in  one of his streams go up up up up up  here's the stairs no  i don't know where the stairs is  i will see you all next time this is  dawn signing off and have a good one  [Music]  you

